---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Organizing CSV & Tabular Data — Using AI Agents for Real Tasks"
  description="Have AI help you read tables, organize them, check them, and produce summaries you can keep using. Starting from an event sign-up CSV, you'll practice using Gemini CLI to read local files, run basic statistics, surface data issues, and finally draft a reminder."
  lang="en"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container ai-agent-csv">
      <a class="back-link" href="/">&larr; Back to Home</a>

      <header class="detail-header">
        <h1 class="detail-title">Putting AI Agents to Work on a First Real Task: Tabular Data</h1>
        <p class="detail-subtitle">
          AI workflows aren't about letting the model run wild. They're about <strong>breaking the task into clear steps</strong> and letting AI help inside a safe, checkable boundary. This page applies AI agents to a very common task: <strong>organizing CSV and tabular data</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="version-note">The steps and screens on this page were verified against Gemini CLI v0.54.4.</p>
      </header>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Whether it's an event sign-up sheet, survey results, a course roster, a reading list, or interview notes — most data ends up in a table. <strong>It looks simple, but cleaning it up usually surfaces several problems</strong>:
      </p>

      <ul class="cross-list">
        <li><strong>Inconsistent formatting</strong> across rows</li>
        <li>Some columns are <strong>blank</strong></li>
        <li>The same meaning is written in <strong>different ways</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Column names are unclear</strong></li>
        <li>You want to summarize but <strong>don't know where to start</strong></li>
        <li>You want a digest, but worry the <strong>AI will just make things up</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        So what we're practicing here isn't deep data analysis. It's:
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          Letting AI <strong>read, organize, and check tables</strong>,<br/>
          and produce <strong>summaries you can keep using</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Section: environment check ============ -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Environment check before you start</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Before touching anything, confirm two things. If you skip these, later failures are hard to trace back to their cause.
      </p>

      <ol class="numbered-list">
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">1</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text">Check your <strong>Node.js version</strong>: it needs to be <strong>20 or above</strong>. Below 20 you'll see <code>EBADENGINE</code>.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">2</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text">Check the <strong>PowerShell execution policy</strong>, and look at the <strong>CurrentUser</strong> row.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <pre class="code-block"><code>node -v
Get-ExecutionPolicy -List</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If <strong>CurrentUser</strong> shows <code>Restricted</code> or <code>Undefined</code>, run the line below to set it to <code>RemoteSigned</code>. This does not require administrator rights:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block"><code>Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          If the <code>MachinePolicy</code> or <code>UserPolicy</code> rows are <strong>not</strong> <code>Undefined</code>, they're locked by group policy and the command above will have no effect.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Section: build practice data ============ -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Build a CSV practice file first</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        We'll prepare an "<strong>event sign-up</strong>" sheet. Everything happens in PowerShell — <strong>no particular editor is required</strong>. First create the project folder:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block"><code>cd $HOME
mkdir csv-agent-practice
cd csv-agent-practice
mkdir data</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Avoid creating it at the root of <code>C:\</code> — a standard account may not have write permission there.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Now create the CSV. <strong>Copy the whole block and paste it into PowerShell</strong> — no Excel needed, and no column-structure problems:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block"><code>@'
name,department,session,diet,needs_certificate,notes
Wang Xiaoming,Chinese Y2,Morning,Meat,Yes,
Li Jiaying,History Y3,Afternoon,Vegetarian,No,Wants front row
Chen Boyu,Sociology Y1,Morning,,Yes,Diet not filled
Lin Yijun,Foreign Lang Y4,Afternoon,Meat,Yes,
Zhang Tingxuan,Psychology Y2,Morning,Vegetarian,Yes,Will be 10 min late
Huang Zihao,Communication Y3,Afternoon,Meat,No,
Wu Yating,Education Y1,Morning,Vegetarian,Yes,
Zheng Yuxiang,Law Y2,Afternoon,,No,Diet not filled
'@ | Set-Content -Path "data\activity_signup.csv" -Encoding UTF8</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          If you prefer building the file in Excel yourself: when saving, pick the file type "<strong>CSV UTF-8 (Comma delimited)</strong>", and the data must sit across <strong>columns A to F</strong> — not pasted as whole rows into column A. Then run the self-check below.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Section: self-check after creating the file ============ -->
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Once it's created, confirm the file structure is correct:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block"><code>Get-Content data\activity_signup.csv -TotalCount 2</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If it's right, you'll see two lines with <strong>no double quotes at the start or end</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          If every line is wrapped in <code>"</code>, the file has <strong>only one column</strong>, not six (usually caused by pasting whole rows into column A in Excel). Recreate it with the command above.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Section: API key and model ============ -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Set the API key and model</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Next, create <code>.gemini/.env</code> in the project, put your API key in it, and pin the model this page uses:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block"><code>mkdir .gemini
Set-Content -Path ".gemini\.env" -Value "GEMINI_API_KEY=your_key`nGEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite"
Get-Content .gemini\.env</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          When you edit <code>.env</code> later, <strong>don't use Notepad</strong>. It quietly appends <code>.txt</code> on save, turning the file into <code>.env.txt</code>, and it will no longer be read.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        At this point the folder structure looks like:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block"><code>csv-agent-practice
├─ .gemini
│  └─ .env
└─ data
   └─ activity_signup.csv</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Viewed as a table, the CSV you just created looks like:
      </p>

      <div class="csv-table-wrapper">
        <table class="csv-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Name</th>
              <th>Department</th>
              <th>Session</th>
              <th>Diet</th>
              <th>Needs Cert.</th>
              <th>Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Wang Xiaoming</td><td>Chinese Y2</td><td>Morning</td><td>Meat</td><td>Yes</td><td></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Li Jiaying</td><td>History Y3</td><td>Afternoon</td><td>Vegetarian</td><td>No</td><td>Wants front row</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Chen Boyu</td><td>Sociology Y1</td><td>Morning</td><td class="csv-empty">—</td><td>Yes</td><td>Diet not filled</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Lin Yijun</td><td>Foreign Lang Y4</td><td>Afternoon</td><td>Meat</td><td>Yes</td><td></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Zhang Tingxuan</td><td>Psychology Y2</td><td>Morning</td><td>Vegetarian</td><td>Yes</td><td>Will be 10 min late</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Huang Zihao</td><td>Communication Y3</td><td>Afternoon</td><td>Meat</td><td>No</td><td></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Wu Yating</td><td>Education Y1</td><td>Morning</td><td>Vegetarian</td><td>Yes</td><td></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Zheng Yuxiang</td><td>Law Y2</td><td>Afternoon</td><td class="csv-empty">—</td><td>No</td><td>Diet not filled</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        It's a small dataset, but a good practice fit because it contains several common situations:
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Some people <strong>didn't fill in their diet</strong></li>
        <li>The notes column is <strong>partly empty</strong>, partly contains <strong>extra requests</strong></li>
        <li>You may want to know <strong>how many people are in each session</strong></li>
        <li>You may want to list <strong>who needs an attendance certificate</strong></li>
        <li>You may want AI to help <strong>check whether the data is complete</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- ============ Section: read CSV file ============ -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Let Gemini CLI read a local file</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The exercise is <strong>not</strong> to paste CSV content into the prompt — it's to let Gemini CLI read a local file. Gemini CLI can use <code>@file_path</code> to add a file or folder's contents to the prompt; given a single file path, it reads that file.
      </p>

      <ol class="numbered-list">
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">1</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text">Open PowerShell and <code>cd</code> into <code>csv-agent-practice</code> (<strong>not <code>data</code></strong>).</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">2</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text"><strong>Start Gemini CLI.</strong> Pick one of the two routes below, depending on which machine you're on.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">3</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text">Each exercise below uses this file path: <code>@./data/activity_signup.csv</code>.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="install-grid">
        <article class="install-card">
          <p class="install-tag">ROUTE A</p>
          <h3 class="install-title">Your own laptop</h3>
          <p class="install-desc">
            Install it once, and from then on a single word starts it:
          </p>
          <pre class="code-block"><code>npm install -g @google/gemini-cli
gemini</code></pre>
          <p class="install-desc">
            You may still need to set PATH afterwards, otherwise you'll get "<code>gemini</code> is not recognized".
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="install-card">
          <p class="install-tag">ROUTE B</p>
          <h3 class="install-title">School computer (this page's default)</h3>
          <p class="install-desc">
            No install, no PATH setup, and it still works after a restore-card reset:
          </p>
          <pre class="code-block"><code>npx @google/gemini-cli</code></pre>
          <p class="install-desc">
            Lab machines have restore cards, so anything installed is gone by the next class — reinstalling every session isn't practical. <strong>The syntax after startup is identical</strong> on both routes, sub-commands included.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The first <code>npx</code> run downloads and caches the package — <strong>about 30 seconds in testing</strong> (single user, normal network). A restore card wipes that cache too, so the first launch of every class has to download again, and a whole class downloading at once will be slow.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        A few things worth being clear about regarding <code>@</code>:
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><code>@</code> is <strong>Gemini CLI's file-reading syntax, unrelated to VS Code</strong> — it works the same in any terminal</li>
        <li>Whether the file can be read depends only on <strong>which folder the CLI was started from</strong></li>
        <li>The backslash form <code>@data\activity_signup.csv</code> <strong>also works</strong> — either is fine</li>
        <li>Typing <code>@</code> opens a path autocomplete menu. If the menu blocks the screen after you paste a prompt, press <strong>Esc</strong> to close it, then submit</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <code>@./data/activity_signup.csv</code> means: please ask Gemini CLI to read <code>activity_signup.csv</code> in the <code>data</code> folder under the current project directory.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Section: first launch screens ============ -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">What you'll see on the first launch</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        A few things appear in sequence the first time you start. Knowing what's coming makes it less confusing.
      </p>

      <ol class="numbered-list">
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">1</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text"><strong>First npx run</strong>: the screen sits on downloading and caching the package, about 30 seconds in testing. You may see <code>Ok to proceed? (y)</code> along the way.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">2</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text"><strong>"Do you trust the files in this folder?"</strong>: three options (trust this folder / trust the parent folder / don't trust). <strong>You must pick <code>1. Trust folder</code></strong>.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">3</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text"><strong>Authentication screen</strong>: when <code>.gemini/.env</code> holds a valid key, this screen <strong>does not appear</strong>.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">4</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text"><strong>Confirm the model</strong>: check the <strong>status bar in the bottom-right corner</strong> — it should read <code>gemini-3.5-flash-lite</code>.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/CSV練習資料夾信任提示.png"
          alt="Gemini CLI starting for the first time inside csv-agent-practice, showing the version, API-key authentication, and the three folder-trust options"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Figure 1. The first-launch screen. The top shows Gemini CLI v0.54.4 and Authenticated with gemini-api-key (with a key present, no auth menu appears), and above the trust prompt: Skipping project agents due to untrusted folder.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <code>Skipping project agents due to untrusted folder.</code> means exactly this: <strong>until you choose, project-level settings are not loaded</strong>. Pick "Don't trust" and neither <code>.env</code> nor your MCP settings come through. The screen itself spells it out — trusting a folder means allowing it to load local configuration and run actions on your behalf.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Normally you <strong>won't see the authentication screen</strong>. <strong>If it does appear</strong>, <code>.env</code> wasn't read — it may be missing, in the wrong path, renamed to <code>.env.txt</code>, or you chose not to trust the folder in the previous step. Go back and check rather than clicking through. (Also, don't pick Sign in with Google — that option has been unavailable for free personal accounts since 2026/6/18.)
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>Don't type <code>/model</code>.</strong> It switches you to Auto, which routes to <code>gemini-3.1-pro-preview</code> (0 quota) and <code>gemini-3.5-flash</code> (20 RPD) — neither is usable in class.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Part: practice ============ -->
      <div class="part-divider">
        <span class="part-label">PRACTICE</span>
        <span class="part-text">Prompt Exercises</span>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Run the four exercises below in order. <strong>At each step, confirm the AI got it right</strong> before moving on. If something looks off, stop and fix it — don't rush ahead.
      </p>

      <!-- Exercise 1 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">EXERCISE 1</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">First, make sure the AI understands the data</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The goal is to confirm whether the AI has actually understood the data. <strong>Don't rush into counting people or producing announcements yet.</strong> Confirm three things first:
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Did the AI <strong>read the right file</strong>?</li>
        <li>Does it <strong>understand the column names</strong>?</li>
        <li>Does it know <strong>what questions this dataset can answer</strong>?</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If the AI misreads a column up front, every later step inherits that mistake. So <strong>once you have the response, go back and check it against the original CSV item by item</strong> — is the column it calls blank actually blank? Are the people it names the right ones?
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>Observed in testing:</strong> if the CSV's structure is broken (for example, whole rows pasted into column A in Excel), the AI will claim "<strong>the session column has blanks</strong>" — when that column is full from top to bottom. The response looks well-organized and neatly formatted, and the first three points are all correct; only the fourth is fabricated.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/CSV練習1修檔前幻覺.png"
          alt="The AI response when the file structure is broken: point four claims the session or diet columns are blank and cites rows 4 and 8"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Figure 2. The response before the file was fixed. The first three points are correct; only the fourth claims the "session" or "diet" columns are blank and points at "rows 4 and 8" — but the session column is full throughout, and the row numbers are wrong too. (Screenshots on this page come from the Chinese-language run of the same exercise.)</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This is exactly why step one is a verification step. <strong>It isn't that the AI isn't smart — the data structure it received was wrong</strong>, so all it could do was guess from text alignment.
      </p>

      <p class="prompt-label">Prompt example</p>
      <blockquote class="prompt-example">
        Please read @./data/activity_signup.csv.<br/><br/>
        Don't analyze counts yet, and don't produce any announcement.<br/><br/>
        Please summarize:<br/>
        1. What columns does this file contain?<br/>
        2. What does each column mean?<br/>
        3. What kinds of questions is this data suitable for answering?<br/>
        4. Where might human verification be needed?<br/><br/>
        Answer based only on the file contents — don't invent data that isn't there.
      </blockquote>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/CSV練習1實測回覆.png"
          alt="After the CSV was corrected to six columns, the same prompt's response names Chen Boyu and Zheng Yuxiang as the ones with a blank diet column"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Figure 3. After the CSV was fixed to six proper columns, the same prompt and the same model. Point four now names the genuinely blank column — "diet" (Chen Boyu, Zheng Yuxiang). Compared with Figure 2, the difference isn't the model; it's the data structure.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Note the last item under point 2 — the first five columns each state their column name, and only "<strong>notes</strong>" drops it. The content is right, but the format slipped. Small drifts like this are common on lightweight models, and they're the reason to read each step before moving on.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The point of this response isn't depth of analysis — it's <strong>confirming whether the AI has actually understood the data structure</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Exercise 2 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">EXERCISE 2</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Run basic statistics</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The goal is to have the AI run <strong>simple categorical counts</strong> on the CSV — how many people per session, how many vegetarians, who needs a certificate. This step turns one flat table into <strong>something easier to read at a glance</strong>.
      </p>

      <p class="prompt-label">Prompt example</p>
      <blockquote class="prompt-example">
        Please read @./data/activity_signup.csv and produce basic statistics from the file contents.<br/><br/>
        Please answer:<br/>
        1. How many people in the morning session? List names.<br/>
        2. How many in the afternoon session? List names.<br/>
        3. How many meat-eaters? List names.<br/>
        4. How many vegetarians? List names.<br/>
        5. How many people have a blank or missing diet? List names.<br/>
        6. How many people need a certificate? List names.<br/><br/>
        Please present the results in a table.<br/>
        Use only the contents of the file — don't add anything or guess.
      </blockquote>

      <details class="details-card">
        <summary class="details-card__summary">
          <span class="details-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <span class="details-card__title">Possible response</span>
          <span class="details-card__hint">Click to expand a sample response</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="details-card__body">
          <h4 class="example-heading">Basic statistics</h4>

          <div class="csv-table-wrapper">
            <table class="csv-table csv-table--stat">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Statistic</th><th>Count</th><th>Names</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>1. Morning session</td><td class="csv-num">4</td><td>Wang Xiaoming, Chen Boyu, Zhang Tingxuan, Wu Yating</td></tr>
                <tr><td>2. Afternoon session</td><td class="csv-num">4</td><td>Li Jiaying, Lin Yijun, Huang Zihao, Zheng Yuxiang</td></tr>
                <tr><td>3. Meat</td><td class="csv-num">3</td><td>Wang Xiaoming, Lin Yijun, Huang Zihao</td></tr>
                <tr><td>4. Vegetarian</td><td class="csv-num">3</td><td>Li Jiaying, Zhang Tingxuan, Wu Yating</td></tr>
                <tr><td>5. Diet blank or not filled</td><td class="csv-num">2</td><td>Chen Boyu, Zheng Yuxiang</td></tr>
                <tr><td>6. Needs certificate</td><td class="csv-num">5</td><td>Wang Xiaoming, Chen Boyu, Lin Yijun, Zhang Tingxuan, Wu Yating</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          This dataset has only 8 rows, so the counts will usually be right. But <strong>as row counts grow, the risk of the AI miscounting rises — and the mistakes aren't necessarily visible</strong>, because the response stays just as neatly formatted. Before any official use, verify with a spreadsheet or script as well.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Exercise 3 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">EXERCISE 3</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Surface the data issues that need handling</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The goal is to have the AI help <strong>check whether the data is complete</strong>. Cleaning up a table is rarely just about counting — it's also about finding:
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Where it's <strong>blank</strong></li>
        <li>What needs to be <strong>verified</strong></li>
        <li>Which fields will <strong>affect later arrangements</strong></li>
        <li>Which gaps the <strong>AI must not fill in by itself</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The point isn't to ask the AI to modify data — it's to <strong>have the AI flag issues so a human can verify them</strong>.
      </p>

      <p class="prompt-label">Prompt example</p>
      <blockquote class="prompt-example">
        Please read @./data/activity_signup.csv and check this dataset for anything that needs human verification.<br/><br/>
        Please specifically check:<br/>
        1. Which columns are blank?<br/>
        2. Which entries could affect later arrangements?<br/>
        3. Whose data needs to be re-confirmed?<br/>
        4. Which gaps must NOT be filled in by AI?<br/><br/>
        Please answer in this format:<br/><br/>
        　Data issues:<br/>
        　People to verify:<br/>
        　Possible impact:<br/>
        　Recommended next step:<br/><br/>
        Don't modify the original file, and don't fill in answers for blank fields.
      </blockquote>

      <details class="details-card">
        <summary class="details-card__summary">
          <span class="details-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <span class="details-card__title">Possible response</span>
          <span class="details-card__hint">Click to expand a sample response</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="details-card__body">
          <h4 class="example-heading">Data issues</h4>
          <ol class="numbered-list">
            <li>
              <span class="numbered-list__num">1</span>
              <span class="numbered-list__text">The diet column has blanks (Chen Boyu, Zheng Yuxiang).</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="numbered-list__num">2</span>
              <span class="numbered-list__text">The notes column contains supplementary information (Li Jiaying wants the front row, Zhang Tingxuan will be 10 minutes late) as well as prompts about unfilled diet fields (Chen Boyu's "Diet not filled", Zheng Yuxiang's "Diet not filled").</span>
            </li>
          </ol>

          <h4 class="example-heading">People to verify</h4>
          <p class="detail-paragraph">Chen Boyu, Zheng Yuxiang, Li Jiaying, Zhang Tingxuan</p>

          <h4 class="example-heading">Possible impact</h4>
          <ol class="numbered-list">
            <li>
              <span class="numbered-list__num">1</span>
              <span class="numbered-list__text">Catering: with Chen Boyu's and Zheng Yuxiang's diet fields blank, leaving them unconfirmed could lead to the wrong meal count or the wrong meat/vegetarian split.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="numbered-list__num">2</span>
              <span class="numbered-list__text">Venue and flow: Li Jiaying wants the front row and Zhang Tingxuan will be 10 minutes late; without advance attention this could affect on-site seat reservation and the check-in process.</span>
            </li>
          </ol>

          <h4 class="example-heading">Recommended next step</h4>
          <p class="detail-paragraph">Have the organizer contact the relevant people directly (Chen Boyu and Zheng Yuxiang to confirm dietary preference; Li Jiaying to confirm the seating request; Zhang Tingxuan to confirm check-in and late-arrival timing), and have the organizer update or record the data manually after verification.</p>

          <h4 class="example-heading">Which gaps must not be filled in by AI</h4>
          <ol class="numbered-list">
            <li>
              <span class="numbered-list__num">1</span>
              <span class="numbered-list__text">Diet: the AI cannot know Chen Boyu's and Zheng Yuxiang's actual dietary habits (meat, vegetarian, or some other special requirement).</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="numbered-list__num">2</span>
              <span class="numbered-list__text">On-site special requests: the AI cannot decide on the organizer's behalf whether Li Jiaying can be seated in the front row, or assess the real impact of Zhang Tingxuan's 10-minute delay on the event flow.</span>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>Question for class:</strong> "People to verify" lists four names, but Li Jiaying's and Zhang Tingxuan's data is actually complete. Why do they need verifying too?
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          The AI should <strong>not fill in blanks on its own</strong>.<br/>
          It should <strong>flag the issues and remind the user to verify</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This mirrors a workflow principle from earlier: <strong>when data is missing, stop. Don't guess</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- Exercise 4 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">EXERCISE 4</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Turn the data into a draft event reminder</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The previous three exercises walked through "<strong>understand → count → flag</strong>." Now we can have the AI draft an event reminder from the data — but <strong>still not publish anything directly</strong>. The safer pattern is:
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Have the AI produce a <strong>draft</strong></li>
        <li>Have the AI list a <strong>data summary</strong></li>
        <li>Have the AI surface <strong>what still needs human verification</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Whether to publish remains a human decision.
      </p>

      <p class="prompt-label">Prompt example</p>
      <blockquote class="prompt-example">
        Please read @./data/activity_signup.csv and produce a pre-event reminder draft based on the file contents.<br/><br/>
        Please follow these rules:<br/>
        1. Use only data that appears in the CSV.<br/>
        2. Don't publicly list every participant's name.<br/>
        3. Remind participants to attend the session they registered for.<br/>
        4. Remind people whose diet is blank to fill it in soon.<br/>
        5. Remind people who need a certificate to follow the organizer's instructions afterward.<br/>
        6. This is a draft only — don't publish it on my behalf.<br/>
        7. At the end, list what still needs human verification.<br/><br/>
        Please answer in this format:<br/><br/>
        　Reminder draft:<br/>
        　Data summary:<br/>
        　Items needing human verification:
      </blockquote>

      <details class="details-card">
        <summary class="details-card__summary">
          <span class="details-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <span class="details-card__title">Possible response</span>
          <span class="details-card__hint">Click to expand a sample response</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="details-card__body">
          <h4 class="example-heading">Reminder draft</h4>
          <blockquote class="prompt-example">
            Hello everyone,<br/>
            Thank you for registering for this event! Please note the following points:<br/>
            1. <strong>Attend your registered session</strong>: please be sure to attend the session you registered for (morning or afternoon) and arrive on time.<br/>
            2. <strong>Reminder for unfilled diet fields</strong>: some registrants have not yet filled in their dietary requirement (the notes column also shows unfilled cases). Please contact the organizer as soon as possible to complete it, so catering can be counted correctly.<br/>
            3. <strong>Certificate collection</strong>: participants who checked "needs certificate" can collect it after the event according to the organizer's arrangements.<br/>
            4. <strong>On-site notes</strong>: if you raised a seating request or expect to arrive late, please explain it to the on-site staff at check-in.
          </blockquote>

          <h4 class="example-heading">Data summary</h4>
          <ul class="check-list">
            <li>The event is split into a "morning session" and an "afternoon session".</li>
            <li>Participants' dietary requirements include meat and vegetarian, and some have not yet filled it in.</li>
            <li>Some participants have checked that they need a participation certificate.</li>
            <li>Some participants raised personal requests such as seating or late arrival in the notes column.</li>
          </ul>

          <h4 class="example-heading">Items needing human verification</h4>
          <ol class="numbered-list">
            <li><span class="numbered-list__num">1</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Participants whose diet field is blank or unfilled (such as Chen Boyu and Zheng Yuxiang) need the organizer to contact them and confirm their actual dietary preference.</span></li>
            <li><span class="numbered-list__num">2</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Participants who raised personal requests (such as Li Jiaying wanting the front row, and Zhang Tingxuan arriving 10 minutes late) need the organizer to confirm on-site arrangements and feasibility.</span></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Names appearing in the last section is fine — that section is an <strong>internal note for the organizer</strong> and won't be published. What goes out is the "reminder draft" above, and that has to stay clean. <strong>The AI telling those two sections apart is what this exercise is really testing.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          This exercise has stepped into <strong>"draft-style workflows."</strong> The AI is no longer just counting — it's <strong>turning organized table data into usable text</strong>. The final review still belongs to a human.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Section: troubleshoot ============ -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">If Gemini CLI can't find the file</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Sometimes Gemini CLI will report that it <strong>can't find the file</strong>, or it reads something but the answer looks off. In that case, check a few things:
      </p>

      <ol class="numbered-list">
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">1</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text">Confirm the file <strong>actually exists</strong>: <code>csv-agent-practice/data/activity_signup.csv</code>.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">2</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text">Confirm you <strong>started Gemini CLI from inside</strong> <code>csv-agent-practice</code>.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">3</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text">Confirm the path in your prompt is <code>@./data/activity_signup.csv</code>.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">4</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text">If the folder or filename <strong>contains spaces</strong>, avoid them, or escape the path according to the tool's rules.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">5</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text"><strong>The AI can read the file but reports column problems that don't exist</strong>: check the CSV structure with <code>Get-Content data\activity_signup.csv -TotalCount 2</code> and see whether each line is wrapped in double quotes.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="numbered-list__num">6</span>
          <span class="numbered-list__text"><strong>An authentication menu appears at startup</strong>: <code>.env</code> wasn't read. Go back and check the key setup and the folder-trust step.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          When starting out, stick to <strong>letters, numbers, underscores, or hyphens</strong> for folder and file names — e.g. <code>data</code>, <code>activity_signup.csv</code>, <code>course_feedback.csv</code>, <code>reading_list.csv</code>. Fewer surprises that way.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Section: takeaway ============ -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">The most important idea on this page</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Organizing CSV and tabular data is <strong>not "hand the data to AI and let it freestyle."</strong> A more reliable approach is:
      </p>

      <ol class="numbered-list">
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">1</span><span class="numbered-list__text">First, have the AI <strong>read the file</strong></span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">2</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Then, have it <strong>understand the columns</strong></span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">3</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Next, run <strong>basic statistics</strong></span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">4</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Then, <strong>flag the data issues</strong></span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">5</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Only at the end, produce a <strong>summary or draft</strong></span></li>
      </ol>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The benefit: <strong>every step is checkable</strong>. If column understanding is wrong at step 1, you stop right there. If counting goes sideways, you can roll back. When data is missing, don't let the AI guess.
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>The table is the <strong>source of truth</strong></li>
        <li>The AI is an <strong>organizing assistant</strong></li>
        <li>Don't <strong>casually modify the original data</strong></li>
        <li>The final call still belongs to <strong>a human</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <div class="highlight-card highlight-card--final">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          AI is good at helping organize tabular data,<br/>
          but its job is to <strong>assist</strong>, not to <strong>replace</strong>.<br/>
          The final decision still rests with <strong>a person</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

